  "id": "COMPOUND:138 - PROTEIN:585",
  "verbalization": "Pyridoxal, a derivative of vitamin B6, appears to be a target for multiple compounds that ultimately interact with Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH), a key enzyme in glycolytic metabolism. The evidence suggests that Pyridoxal is linked to Tumor necrosis factor receptor superfamily member 10B, which in turn is a target of various compounds, including 4-Ethyl-3-Methyl-N-[2-[4-[(4-Methylcyclohexyl)Carbamoylsulfamoyl]Phenyl]Ethyl]-5-Oxo-2H-Pyrrole-1-Carboxamide and (+)-Verticillol. These compounds, in turn, target GAPDH, indicating a potential pathway where Pyridoxal indirectly influences GAPDH activity through its interaction with the receptor. The presence of multiple distinct compounds targeting GAPDH, all of which share a common target of Tumor necrosis factor receptor superfamily member 10B, suggests a broader mechanism of action involving receptor-mediated signaling. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of receptor activation in modulating GAPDH activity, potentially through downstream metabolic effects.",
